S.append(title(
    EYE, 'Connected\ninteractions', 'Networks, protocols and application messages',
    notes='120-minute lecture plus a separate 120-minute tutorial. Theory-first route: 0-8 opening, 8-15 roles/communication, 15-29 layers/links/IP, 29-40 delivery/transport, 40-49 endpoints, 49-60 diagnosis/discussion, 60-70 break, 70-80 application protocols, 80-94 APIs/REST, 94-103 WebSocket excerpts, 103-115 framing/state/failure, 115-120 tutorial handoff. This content supports the later final-course quiz. There is no end-of-lecture quiz today. The detailed Scene implementation belongs to the separate tutorial. Reuse the Week 4 API and Week 7 object knowledge. Students trace messages, distinguish local/shared state and explain what an absent acknowledgement proves. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/6e5637f56c1179f64d170dc29eecbf312bb41a2f/week09'))
S.append(question('short_answer', 'Ask me anything about the group project',
    hint='Post one question your team wants to resolve.', eyebrow_text='OPENING / GROUP PROJECT AMA',
    notes='0-5 minutes. Open the existing ClassPoint Short Answer activity so students can post project questions. Invite one question per team or individual, then group related questions and answer the most shared ones. Scope, teamwork, requirements, technical choices and current blockers are all welcome. Keep unresolved project-specific questions for the allocated tutorial support time. Do not invent assessment policy: use the current project brief and tutor-confirmed schedule. If ClassPoint is unavailable, collect the same questions verbally or on paper rather than introducing a new external form. Do not publish private student data or identify students in the public deck. This is an ungraded AMA, not a new quiz.'))
S.append(two_col('RECAP / PREVIOUS WEEKS', 'Your team has a workflow and a sensing input', ['Agile / Scrum: plan a usable increment.', 'GitHub Projects: make work visible.', 'Pull requests: review and test changes.', '', 'Keep the next interaction small enough to check.'], ['MediaPipe: turn a camera frame into landmarks.', 'Map a fingertip coordinate to an action.', '', 'Today: send that action to another process.', 'The receiver could run on another device.'],
    notes='5-7 minutes. This bridge follows the actual Week 8 material: Agile/Scrum, GitHub tracking, branch/PR/review workflow and MediaPipe hand landmarks. Do not imply that networking replaces this workflow or that every group must add it. A team can divide a sensor producer and a visual receiver across modules, processes or machines, but must agree their message contract and test the integration. Ask students to reuse their next usable increment rather than invent a new networking project. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/teaching/blob/da0e86c67172c765ab5d7de9c5dfe1f625370667/deck/week08.py .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(content('TODAY / CONNECTED INTERACTIONS', 'How does a gesture reach another program?', ['MediaPipe supplies a fingertip position.', 'A message describes the intended action.', 'Layers, addresses and ports carry it to a service.', 'The receiving application interprets it and replies.', 'Commands help us locate the boundary when it fails.', 'Next week: the app connects to databases and resources.'],
    notes='7-8 minutes. Establish one coherent motivating example for the theory that follows. A normalized x coordinate could become a bounded set_x action; the tutorial uses a slider as a deterministic stand-in, so camera access is not required. Explain that we will study OSI/TCP-IP, delivery modes, diagnostic commands and advanced API patterns before applying them in the separate tutorial. Networking is useful only if the project needs a boundary between processes or devices. This week the client talks to an application through an API/WebSocket. Next week the application talks to databases and other resources. A network database commonly uses a TCP socket plus its database protocol; the socket is an endpoint, not the protocol itself. Embedded SQLite is a useful exception and does not require a network server. Do not teach database setup here. The final-course quiz later can assess the concepts taught here; there is no end-of-lecture quiz today.'))
S.append(figure_slide('NETWORKS / POSSIBLE CONNECTIONS', 'More participants, more possible connections', F.network_growth(),
    notes='8-9 minutes. Reintroduce the source lecture network-effects question with a shared drawing or multiplayer installation. For n participants, n(n-1)/2 counts possible undirected pairs if every pair can connect. It does not measure value, traffic or engagement, and real networks need not connect every pair directly. Metcalfe\'s law is a useful hypothesis about network value, not a universal experimentally proven formula. Ask whether an empty chat group becomes useful merely by adding silent accounts. Source crosswalk: supplied 2025 PDF slide 24. The pair count is a combinatorial derivation, not an economic estimate.'))
S.append(two_col('ROLES / PROCESSES', 'Client and server are roles', ['Client: initiates an exchange.', 'Server: listens for incoming requests.', '', 'Two programs can share one laptop.', 'One program may play both roles.'], ['Gallery browser requests artwork.', 'Python service returns metadata.', '', 'A peer can also serve other peers.', 'The role describes this exchange.'],
    notes='9-11 minutes. Ask students to identify the initiating process and the listening process in the Week 4 example. A server need not be a large remote computer. Two browser tabs are separate views, and the Python process is a third participant. Peer-to-peer describes a different organization of roles, not the absence of protocols or addresses. Distinguish source files, running processes and physical devices. The same laptop can host the browser and service during development and different machines later.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('COMMUNICATION / AGREED RULES', 'Communication rules and evidence', ['Simplex: one direction.', 'Half duplex: take turns.', 'Full duplex: both directions can overlap.', '', 'A protocol defines message rules.'], ['Reachability: can this endpoint be reached?', 'Routing: which next hop carries the packet?', '', 'TCP ACK: bytes reached the peer TCP.', 'App reply: the program reports an outcome.'],
    notes='11-15 minutes. A broadcast-only display, walkie-talkie conversation and telephone call are useful direction analogies, not claims about their exact network stacks. Full duplex is a capability: an application can still choose a send-then-wait loop. Both sides need compatible formats, ordering and meaning. Reachability also depends on listener binding, routes and firewall policy, not just a valid IP address. A TCP acknowledgement does not prove Scene.apply ran or that a value reached storage. Predict whether a server can receive bytes and crash before replying. Source: RFC 9293 sections 2.2, 3.1 and 3.8, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(figure_slide('LAYER MODELS', 'Seven OSI layers, four Internet layers', F.osi_layers(),
    notes='15-21 minutes. Walk upward: physical signals, local-link frames, routed IP packets, transport endpoints, then conversation/representation/application responsibilities. Explain each job with the control message: physical encodes bits as signals; data link frames local-hop traffic; network addresses/routes packets across networks; transport connects process endpoints and supplies its chosen delivery service; session organizes dialogues; presentation handles representation/encoding; application defines the service operations. This reference model does not require seven separate libraries. Do not merely recite a mnemonic. The diagram groups session/presentation/application into the Internet application layer and data-link/physical into link. This is an approximate teaching mapping between models, not a rule that every real protocol occupies exactly one box. In our app TCP carries WebSocket frames containing JSON, while loopback avoids a physical cable. TLS is encryption between application and transport, not a reason to claim HTTPS is a new physical layer. Sources: ITU-T X.200, https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.200-199407-I/en ; RFC 1122 section 1.1.3, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 . Ask which layer needs changing if the same JSON travels over Ethernet instead of Wi-Fi.'))
S.append(figure_slide('ENCAPSULATION', 'Each layer carries the next layer\'s data', F.encapsulation(),
    notes='21-23 minutes. Trace one JSON action down the stack: WebSocket frames messages, TCP carries ordered bytes, IP routes packets and the link carries a frame to the next hop. Headers add information for that layer. At the receiver each layer interprets its own header before passing payload upward. A WebSocket message can itself span frames, and TCP can split or combine those bytes across segments; this is a conceptual nesting, not a promise of one message per packet. At a router the link envelope changes while the IP packet continues toward its destination. HTTPS/WSS adds TLS protection; the simplified local ws example omits it. Sources: RFC 1122 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 ; RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 . Ask which piece contains set_x and which identifies TCP port 8009.'))
S.append(two_col('LINKS / PHYSICAL CHOICES', 'Distance, data rate and power', ['Ethernet: a wired local network.', 'USB: nearby host and devices.', 'I2C / 1-Wire: component or sensor buses.', '', 'A cable does not imply an Internet service.'], ['Wi-Fi / 5G: wireless network access.', 'Bluetooth: nearby devices.', 'LoRa: long-range, small messages.', '', 'Compare power, range and useful data rate.'],
    notes='23-25 minutes. Retain the historical wired/wireless examples without pretending they have one fixed speed or range. Versions, environment, topology and configuration matter. Ethernet and Wi-Fi commonly carry IP; USB, I2C and 1-Wire are not interchangeable IP network technologies. A sensor bus can feed a gateway that exposes an API. LoRa is a radio modulation technology; LoRaWAN adds networking. Ask whether a room-scale high-rate video stream and a battery-powered distant temperature sensor should make the same link choice. No numerical throughput claims or hardware setup are required. Primary references: IEEE 802 LAN/MAN overview https://www.ieee802.org/ ; Bluetooth technology overview https://www.bluetooth.com/learn-about-bluetooth/tech-overview/ ; LoRa Alliance https://lora-alliance.org/about-lorawan/ .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('IP CONFIGURATION', 'An address is only part of the configuration', ['IPv4: 32-bit addresses.', 'Example: 192.168.1.23', 'IPv6: 128-bit addresses.', 'Example: 2001:db8::23', '', 'One device can have several addresses.'], ['Prefix / netmask: which network?', 'Gateway: where to send off-link traffic?', 'DNS resolver: where to ask about names?', '', 'DHCP can supply IPv4 configuration.', 'IPv6 also supports auto-configuration.'],
    notes='25-27 minutes. A network interface may have several addresses, and addresses can change. The IPv6 example uses the documentation prefix, not a live destination. For IPv4, a /24 prefix corresponds to 255.255.255.0. DHCP can lease an address and supply configuration such as router and DNS information. IPv6 uses its own mechanisms, including SLAAC and DHCPv6; do not teach all configuration as IPv4 DHCP. A DNS server and a default gateway do different jobs even when a home router provides both. Loopback 127.0.0.1 and ::1 stay on this host. Sources: RFC 791 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791 ; RFC 8200 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200 ; RFC 2131 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131 ; RFC 4862 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4862 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(figure_slide('ROUTING / NEXT HOP', 'Same subnet or through the gateway?', F.host_route(),
    notes='27-29 minutes. Work the illustrated IPv4 /24 example. The local route covers 192.168.1.0/24, so 192.168.1.42 is on-link. A destination outside this local prefix uses the configured default route if no more specific route matches. The packet keeps its final IP destination while the local-link frame goes to the next-hop gateway. Routers choose a next hop; they do not carry one unchanging Ethernet frame across the entire Internet. We simplify to one active interface and one default route. Ask which setting students would inspect when local devices work but off-network destinations fail. Source: RFC 1122 section 3.3.1, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('PACKET DELIVERY / 1', 'Unicast: one addressed recipient', F.packet_delivery('unicast'),
    notes='29-31 minutes. The highlighted receiver is one IP destination. Several clients can each have their own unicast connection to a server. The presence of several machines on the same network does not make every packet intended for all of them. Ask which receiver should handle this addressed message. Source: RFC 1122, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('PACKET DELIVERY / 2', 'Broadcast: all on a local scope', F.packet_delivery('broadcast'),
    notes='31-33 minutes. Broadcast targets all recipients in a defined broadcast scope, not everyone on the Internet. A local IPv4 broadcast or Ethernet broadcast has a bounded scope; routers generally do not forward ordinary local broadcasts. IPv6 has no broadcast addresses and uses multicast for relevant functions. Distinguish this network operation from a Python function named broadcast that loops over separate connections. Sources: RFC 1122 section 3.3.6, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122 ; RFC 4291 section 2, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4291 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('PACKET DELIVERY / 3', 'Multicast: members of an addressed group', F.packet_delivery('multicast'),
    notes='33-35 minutes. A multicast group address identifies participating recipients. Delivery depends on membership, network support and scope; it is not a promise that any arbitrary remote group will receive a packet. Compare unicast to one, broadcast to all within scope and multicast to a group. Our browser WebSocket scene uses one connection per client, not IP multicast. Later broadcast() is application fanout over those separate unicast TCP connections. Source: RFC 1112, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1112 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('TRANSPORT / CONNECTION SETUP', 'TCP starts with a three-way handshake', F.tcp_handshake(),
    notes='35-38 minutes. A client sends SYN, the listening endpoint returns SYN+ACK, and the client acknowledges. This establishes sequence-number synchronization for a connection; it is not one handshake for every application message. TCP then carries ordered bytes in both directions, with sequence numbers, acknowledgements and retransmission. Loss can delay later bytes and a connection can eventually fail. Do not repeat the source deck claims that TCP is error-immune or always needs three times as many packets. TLS and the WebSocket opening handshake are additional protocols, not these three TCP messages. Source: RFC 9293 section 3.5, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .'))
S.append(two_col('TRANSPORT / DESIGN CHOICES', 'TCP streams and UDP datagrams', ['TCP: ordered, reliable byte stream.', 'Retransmission may add delay.', 'Application messages need framing.', '', 'An applied action still needs app-level evidence.'], ['UDP: separate datagrams.', 'No delivery or order guarantee in UDP.', 'No TCP-style connection handshake.', '', 'Fresh positions may replace older updates.'],
    notes='38-40 minutes. Reliable means TCP works to deliver an ordered byte stream or reports failure, not that all applications always succeed. UDP preserves a datagram boundary but an application may need sequence numbers, loss handling and congestion control. A video call may prefer timely media to waiting for every obsolete sample; a coin transaction needs careful application semantics even over TCP. UDP does not automatically make an application faster. QUIC is an example of reliable transport built over UDP, so HTTP/3 does not disprove these UDP-layer guarantees. Sources: RFC 9293 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 ; RFC 768 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc768 ; RFC 9114 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(figure_slide('PORTS / ENDPOINTS', 'One listening port, many connections', F.endpoint_connections(),
    notes='40-43 minutes. A socket is a communication endpoint used by a program; the application protocol defines the rules and meaning of its messages. TCP and UDP have separate 16-bit port namespaces. A TCP connection is distinguished by its local and remote address/port pairs. The listening server can accept many clients on TCP 8009 while each client uses its own source port. Both HTTP routes and /ws can share this listener because the application dispatches the path/protocol. Conventional ports do not permanently reserve an application identity; here we choose 8009 to avoid the presenter on 8765, and raw TCP uses 8010. Do not repeat the historical one-port-one-connection claim. Source: RFC 9293 sections 2.2 and 3.1, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('ADDRESS', 'Four parts of one destination', F.address(),
    notes='http is the application scheme; 127.0.0.1 means this laptop; 8009 selects the listening service; the application interprets /state. For a hostname, the resolver may use local configuration, cache or DNS. A numeric IP literal needs no DNS lookup. localhost normally resolves locally. A port is an endpoint number, not a permanent application name. The official presenter uses 8765; the later raw TCP observation uses 8010.'))
S.append(two_col('HOST / BIND ADDRESS', 'Localhost means this computer', ['Client destination:', 'ws://127.0.0.1:8009/ws', 'The current laptop, TCP 8009, route /ws.', '', 'A hostname may need DNS resolution.'], ['Server bind: 127.0.0.1', 'Only local connections in this demo.', '', '0.0.0.0 binds all IPv4 interfaces.', 'A remote client uses the server\'s reachable IP.'],
    notes='46-49 minutes, following the URL figure at 43-46. Trace scheme, host resolution, IP, TCP port, WebSocket handshake/path, then JSON messages. localhost on a phone refers to that phone, not the instructor laptop. Binding and client destination are different settings: 0.0.0.0 is a wildcard bind address, not the address to send to. The current app deliberately binds 127.0.0.1. A LAN extension needs explicit authorization and rehearsal of network reachability, firewall and access controls; do not simply expose the teaching service. Historical echo uses localhost:8765; its push variant binds 0.0.0.0:8765. Read those sources, but do not launch them alongside the presenter. Source: Python socket documentation https://docs.python.org/3/library/socket.html#socket-families and current pinned server.py.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('DIAGNOSTICS / CONFIGURATION AND SOCKETS', 'Inspect one boundary at a time', ['Windows: ipconfig /all', 'macOS: ifconfig', 'macOS gateway: route -n get default', 'Linux: ip addr; ip route', 'Find the address, prefix and gateway.'], ['Windows / macOS: netstat -an', 'Linux: ss -ltn', 'curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8009/state', '', 'Find a listener, then inspect the HTTP reply.'],
    notes='49-52 minutes. These commands answer different questions: interface configuration, route selection, open/listening sockets, then an application response. ifconfig shows interface addresses, not the default gateway; on macOS use route -n get default for that. netstat exists on many systems but flags/output differ. On modern Linux ip/ss are often preferred; ifconfig/netstat may need legacy packages. Demonstrate only the commands available on the instructor host, not five installations. Look for 127.0.0.1:8009 in LISTEN before requesting /state. An IP configuration alone does not prove a process is listening. Sources: Microsoft ipconfig https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/ipconfig ; Microsoft netstat https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/netstat ; iproute2 https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/network/iproute2/iproute2.git/tree/man/man8 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('DIAGNOSTICS / ACTIVE PROBES AND CAPTURE', 'Nmap and Wireshark answer different questions', ['Nmap probes hosts and ports.', 'Own prepared lab only:', 'nmap -sT -p 8009 127.0.0.1', '', 'A closed port differs from an HTTP 404.'], ['Wireshark inspects captured packets.', 'Own loopback demo traffic only.', 'Filter: tcp.port == 8009', '', 'Captures can contain private content.'],
    notes='52-54 minutes. The optional Nmap command targets only the instructor\'s own loopback listener; no campus or classmate scans. Scan another system only with explicit permission from its owner and within the authorized scope. This is not a required installation. Wireshark can show the handshake and protocol data, but encrypted HTTPS/WSS hides application payloads unless separately decrypted with authorization. Capture only the synthetic demo traffic, close unrelated apps, and do not publish credentials, cookies, student identifiers or raw captures. Use a prepared local capture if live capture is unavailable. Nmap sends probes while Wireshark primarily observes captured traffic. Sources: https://nmap.org/book/man.html ; https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChapterIntroduction.html .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('PREDICT / LOCATE THE FAILURE', 'Wrong route or no listener?', ['Server running:', 'GET /statee', 'HTTP 404 / application answered.', '', 'Server stopped:', 'GET /state', 'Connection failure / no HTTP response.'], ['Same host.', 'Same port.', 'Different failure boundary.', '', 'Read the status and body.', 'A response is not automatically success.'],
    notes='54-56 minutes. Ask for a prediction before requesting /statee from the running server. Compare its 404 with a request to /state while no server is listening. An HTTP error is still a response from an application. A connection failure may have no HTTP response. Read the visible status and body rather than calling every problem an internet outage.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('CHECK / LOCATE THE BOUNDARY', 'Which observation narrows the fault?', ['A: No interface address.', 'B: No listener on TCP 8009.', 'C: HTTP 404 from /statee.', '', 'Which one proves an app answered?'], ['D: HTTP 422 with an error body.', 'E: HTTP 200 with unexpected JSON.', 'F: A view still shows yesterday\'s value.', '', 'Which checks would you make next?'],
    notes='56-60 minutes. Give pairs one minute to reason, then explain their evidence. C proves an HTTP-speaking application answered, though not necessarily the intended route. D proves an app processed and rejected the request. E calls for checking the data contract and service identity. F is ambiguous until we check connection status, freshness and data flow. B identifies a local socket issue, not necessarily a broken network. A may be irrelevant if the demo uses loopback. These are formative discussion prompts, not a new graded quiz or final-course quiz items. Students should justify the boundary instead of guessing a command.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(statement('Break / 10 minutes', size=96,
    notes='60-70 minutes. No tutorial implementation work belongs in this break. Take the full ten-minute break; no new material belongs in it.'))
S.append(two_col('APPLICATION PROTOCOLS', 'Name lookup, web requests and live media', ['DNS: names to records, including IP addresses.', 'HTTP: requests and responses.', 'HTTPS: HTTP over a secure transport.', '', 'Ports 53, 80 and 443 are conventions.'], ['WebSocket: continuing two-way messages.', 'WebRTC: real-time media and data.', '', 'JSON: one possible data representation.', 'REST: an architectural style for APIs.'],
    notes='70-71 minutes. Explain the different jobs rather than a flat list of acronyms. A DNS resolver can return A/AAAA records, but DNS stores other kinds of records too. HTTPS provides encrypted, integrity-protected communication and authentication through TLS, not a guarantee that returned content is truthful. HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 normally use TCP; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP. WebRTC is a family of browser APIs/protocols for peer connections, often requiring signaling and sometimes relay servers, not just another spelling of WebSocket. Our small local control app uses HTTP and WebSocket, not WebRTC. Sources: RFC 1034 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034 ; RFC 9110 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 ; W3C WebRTC https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/ . Ask which mechanism fetches a saved record and which carries a live video call.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('DNS / NAME RESOLUTION', 'A name can lead to several addresses', ['gallery.example -> DNS records', 'A: an IPv4 address', 'AAAA: an IPv6 address', '', 'The resolver can cache earlier answers.'], ['127.0.0.1 is already an address.', 'localhost normally resolves locally.', '', 'DNS success does not prove a listener.', 'An IP response does not prove HTTP success.'],
    notes='71-73 minutes. gallery.example is a reserved example name, not a service we ask students to visit. The resolver may first consult local configuration/cache before contacting DNS. Multiple answers can represent multiple interfaces or service endpoints. DNS can work while a server is stopped, and an IP literal can work while DNS is broken. Ask how using a numeric loopback URL changes the chain of possible failures. DNS maps names to several kinds of records, not only URLs to websites. Source: RFC 1034 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1034 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('HTTP / HTTPS', 'Encryption protects the exchange', ['HTTP: request and response semantics.', 'HTTPS: HTTP with secure transport.', '', 'TLS protects confidentiality and integrity.', 'Certificates help authenticate the peer.'], ['HTTP may reuse a connection.', 'HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP.', 'HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP.', '', 'Encrypted content can still be wrong.'],
    notes='73-76 minutes. Explain why the tiny loopback demo uses http but public services normally use https. Do not send credentials over the plain teaching connection or expose it as a production service. An HTTP connection need not close after every response. HTTPS is not a claim that the website is honest, that every app request succeeded, or that data was saved. The transport-version detail prevents treating every web request as TCP without exception; students need the functional distinction, not version trivia. Sources: RFC 9110 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 8446 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446 ; RFC 9114 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('LIVE COMMUNICATION', 'WebSocket and WebRTC serve different needs', ['WebSocket: app messages over a channel.', 'A chat, shared controller or live dashboard.', '', 'The server may send at any time.', 'The application defines message meaning.'], ['WebRTC: real-time media and data.', 'A call, camera stream or peer data channel.', '', 'Peers need connection setup/signaling.', 'A relay may be needed to reach each other.'],
    notes='76-80 minutes. A two-way connection alone does not make an app a video-call system. WebRTC includes APIs and protocols for media/data, connectivity checks and secure transport. Signaling is coordinated separately and may itself use WebSocket or HTTP; that does not make the media a WebSocket message. WebSocket is simpler for our small JSON control messages. Peer-to-peer paths are not guaranteed because networks and NAT may require a relay. Ask students to choose one for a collaborative slider versus a live remote video critique and justify the traffic needs. Sources: https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/ ; https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('APIS / REQUEST AND RESPONSE', 'An API exposes operations and representations', ['A client sends an agreed request.', 'Method + path + headers + optional body.', '', 'The server returns a status and optional body.', 'JSON is common, but not required.'], ['Weather: request a forecast.', 'Gallery: fetch artwork metadata.', 'Our scene: GET /state.', '', 'API does not mean AI or a remote vendor.'],
    notes='80-83 minutes. Reconnect with the Week 4 tide example and the historical API examples. An application programming interface is a boundary programs use; not every API is an HTTP API. For this lecture trace a network HTTP request into a Python handler, then, when applicable, a representation out to a browser. HEAD and 204 responses have no content; do not assume every response has a JSON body. See RFC 9110 section 6.4.1. A request may require authentication in a real service, but our loopback teaching app uses no account. Separate network reachability, HTTP status, parsed data and application meaning. Source for HTTP request/response semantics: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(figure_slide('HTTP API / TRACE', 'One request, one corresponding response', F.request_response(),
    notes='83-86 minutes. Use this as a short optional live observation: start the prepared current server on 127.0.0.1:8009 and run curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8009/state. No installation or line-by-line Scene implementation in the lecture. Read the status, content type and JSON body; ask where the browser\'s drawing happens after it receives data. If the example is not ready, inspect the documented message shape and label the discussion as a trace, not a live run. This is a read, not a mutation; the detailed two-view demo belongs to the tutorial. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/blob/6e5637f56c1179f64d170dc29eecbf312bb41a2f/week09/server.py .'))
S.append(two_col('REST / HTTP METHODS', 'Resources, representations and consistent operations', ['GET /artworks/7: read a representation.', 'POST /artworks: submit or create.', 'PUT /artworks/7: replace a representation.', 'PATCH /artworks/7: apply a partial change.', 'DELETE /artworks/7: remove the resource.'], ['REST is an architectural style.', 'Each request carries needed context.', 'The server may still store resource data.', '', 'Our /action endpoint is a command API.'],
    notes='86-89 minutes. These gallery URLs are illustrative, not endpoints in the supplied scene server. REST emphasizes resources, representations, a uniform interface and stateless requests; it is not a synonym for every JSON-over-HTTP service. Stateless requests do not mean the server cannot store art records. Replace the old invented UPDATE method with PUT/PATCH and distinguish their semantics. POST can have several application-defined meanings, not only create. GET should not request a mutation. Our small server implements GET /state and POST /action; do not advertise PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes it lacks or claim complete REST conformance. Sources: Fielding chapter 5 https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm ; RFC 9110 section 9 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; RFC 5789 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('HTTP / RESULTS', 'A response is evidence to interpret', ['2xx: request succeeded at the HTTP level.', '4xx: client request needs attention.', '5xx: server could not fulfill it.', '', '404: route/resource not found.'], ['Contract: x is an integer from 0 to 100.', '0 / 100 accepted. 101 rejected.', 'Reject before changing state.', '', '400: malformed JSON. 422: invalid value.', 'No connection: no HTTP status.'],
    notes='89-92 minutes. Status-code classes communicate the result of handling the request. A 200 response still needs interpretation against the application contract; it does not prove the screen updated or persistent storage committed. Use the actual x contract to predict 0, 100 and 101: the first two are accepted, 101 is rejected before mutation. Valid JSON alone does not establish a valid command. This is a rule/result explanation, not the Scene code walkthrough. Specific status choices for malformed JSON and invalid x belong to this server, not every possible framework. Link 404 and missing-listener observations back to the first half. Ask whether a received rejection proves the request never reached the server: no. Sources: RFC 9110 section 15 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110 ; current server.py.', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('STATE / DIFFERENT LIFETIMES', 'Stateless requests can access stored state', ['Request context: what this operation needs.', 'Shared application state: the current scene.', 'Persistent data: survives a process restart.', '', 'REST statelessness concerns request context.'], ['HTTP can read a stored artwork.', 'WebSocket can update a RAM-only scene.', '', 'A persistent connection is not disk storage.', 'A new connection may need a fresh snapshot.'],
    notes='92-94 minutes. Correct the source-deck implication that HTTP/REST means no server-side data. The server can store resources while each request carries the context needed to handle it. Our Scene lives in one Python process and resets on restart, regardless of whether HTTP or WebSocket carries its messages. Persistence is a separate design choice, explored next week with related records. A continuing connection is not the same lifetime as the underlying data. Source: Fielding REST stateless constraint https://ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/rest_arch_style.htm .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(two_col('WEBSOCKET / MESSAGE PATTERN', 'Full duplex permits independent messages', ['Open a connection with a handshake.', 'Send and receive framed messages.', 'Either side can initiate later messages.', '', 'A send-then-receive loop uses only one pattern.'], ['HTTP polling repeats requests.', 'WebSocket can push later changes.', '', 'Define type, data and correlation IDs.', 'Handle closure and reconnect explicitly.'],
    notes='94-95 minutes. Distinguish a persistent bidirectional protocol capability from one echo program\'s chosen sequential loop. WebSocket messages can carry text or binary data; JSON is our application choice. TCP ACK, WebSocket Ping/Pong and application acknowledgement are three different mechanisms. The next three excerpts make this distinction concrete without replacing the theory lecture with a framework tutorial. Source: RFC 6455 https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(code_panel('WEBSOCKET / HISTORICAL CLIENT', 'The connection stays open between messages', ['async with connect("ws://localhost:8765") as websocket:', '    while True:', '        await websocket.send("Hello, world!")', '        response = await websocket.recv()', '        print(response)', '        await asyncio.sleep(1)'], caption='Source excerpt, not a command to start on presenter port 8765.',
    notes=f'95-97 minutes. Actual excerpt from websocket_client_example.py. connect performs the WebSocket opening handshake over a TCP connection and the context keeps it open across loop iterations. This code chooses send then receive then wait, even though WebSocket supports messages in both directions independently. One recv returns one WebSocket message, not an arbitrary TCP byte fragment. Ask whether the server has to close the connection after each reply: no. Trace localhost resolution, TCP 8765, WebSocket, text payload. The historical source uses unpinned dependencies and is not our install route. Read it only; the current tested scene stays on 8009. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_client_example.py ; protocol: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455 .'))
S.append(code_panel('WEBSOCKET / HISTORICAL ECHO SERVER', 'Echo sends an application reply', ['async def echo(websocket):', '    async for message in websocket:', '        print(f"Received message: {message}")', '        await websocket.send(f"You sent: {message}")'], caption='The server replies on the same connection for each incoming message.',
    notes=f'97-99 minutes. Actual echo handler from websocket_server_echo.py. One incoming text message produces one outgoing text message. The surrounding source listens on localhost:8765, conflicting with our presenter, so do not run it in this lecture. Ask what the output is for Hello, world!: You sent: Hello, world! This reply is application data, not the TCP ACK or the WebSocket control Pong. The simple echo handler alone does not demonstrate unsolicited server push, shared state or broadcasting to other clients. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_server_echo.py .'))
S.append(code_panel('WEBSOCKET / HISTORICAL SERVER PUSH', 'The server can send without a new request', ['async def constant_ping(websocket):', '    while True:', '        await asyncio.sleep(5)', '        await websocket.send("Ping!")', '', '# Inside echo(websocket):', 'tasks = [respond(websocket), constant_ping(websocket)]', 'await asyncio.gather(*tasks)'], caption='Here "Ping!" is an ordinary text message, not a protocol Ping frame.',
    notes=f'99-103 minutes. Actual lines from websocket_server_echo_ping.py, with an explanatory comment between the two excerpts. echo() constructs tasks = [respond(websocket), constant_ping(websocket)] and gathers both coroutines. respond handles replies while constant_ping sends text every five seconds. The string Ping! is neither an RFC 6455 Ping control frame nor ICMP ping. The source listens on 0.0.0.0:8765; read it, do not expose it or start it beside the presenter. Predict the original client\'s next recv if Ping! arrives first: it may print the unsolicited message as though it were the reply to its send. A real app needs an independent receive path and message types/IDs. Our current browser does that for state, ack and rejected messages. Source: {ARCHIVE}/websocket_server_echo_ping.py ; control-frame distinction https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-5.5 .'))
S.append(figure_slide('BYTES / MESSAGES', 'One send is not one receive.', F.framing(),
    notes='103-106 minutes. Read the diagram; run the raw TCP example only in the tutorial extension. A buffer accumulates bytes until newline, then extracts a complete message. One read may contain multiple messages or part of one. TCP preserves byte order, not application send boundaries. WebSocket provides message framing before our server parses JSON. Ask what fails if code decodes each recv as one complete color. Framing, reliability and acknowledgement are separate jobs.'))
S.append(two_col('APPLICATION STATE / FANOUT', 'One authoritative scene, several observations', ['A slider is local input until sent.', 'The server applies an accepted action.', 'Each client receives a state snapshot.', '', 'A snapshot describes the scene now.'], ['An ack identifies one applied action.', 'A revision records server order.', '', 'broadcast() sends on each connection.', 'This is not an IP multicast packet.'],
    notes='106-109 minutes. Use the actual tutorial contract without walking through implementation: action {id:a-1, action:set_x, x:25}; state {type:state, x:25, revision:1}; acknowledgement {type:ack, id:a-1, revision:1}. Quote these verbally as fields rather than pretend these unquoted notes are literal JSON. Both clients can observe the same state but only the sender needs its correlated acknowledgement. Revisions order changes within this process lifetime; they are not wall-clock timestamps. The current Python broadcast loop sends separately to each connected WebSocket. Detailed Scene.apply, validation, await, lock and concurrency work is reserved for the tutorial. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/6e5637f56c1179f64d170dc29eecbf312bb41a2f/week09 .', right_font='body', right_size=36, left_size=36, lang=None))
S.append(figure_slide('FAILURE / MISSING APPLICATION REPLY', 'The sender and observer know different things.', F.uncertainty(),
    notes='109-112 minutes. Explain the supplied diagram as a controlled tutorial scenario. In that tutorial, use Send, ignore replies, disconnect with A at 73. This deliberately suppresses the client observation; it is not evidence of random packet loss. B receives the real broadcast and displays 73. A says Unknown because it did not observe its own matching acknowledgement. The tutorial reconnects A and inspects without resending. A missing reply alone establishes neither success nor failure. A repeat set_x can retain the same position but advances revision; a repeated spend could charge twice. An action ID supports correlation, not deduplication, unless the app stores and checks completed IDs.'))
S.append(content('RECOVERY / DISCONNECT AND RECONNECT', 'A new snapshot is not a retry.', ['Disconnect A.', 'Change the scene from B.', 'Reconnect A: inspect the current snapshot.', 'A does not automatically resend its old action.'],
    notes='112-114 minutes. Predict the tutorial sequence: disconnect A, change the scene in B, then reconnect A. Connect makes a new channel and the server sends its current snapshot. A visibly remains disconnected while B works. Reconnection does not resend the old action or establish exactly-once delivery. Callbacks from an old closed channel must not change the new channel state.'))
S.append(cards('TRANSFER / PROTOCOL CHOICES', 'What does this interaction need?', [('EVENT','Spend one coin','Reliable delivery plus application rules.'),('UPDATE','Move an avatar','Frequent position snapshots; freshness matters.'),('READ','Fetch a saved record','HTTP request/response is often enough.')],
    notes='114-115 minutes. UDP can avoid retransmitting obsolete position updates, but delivery and order require application decisions. Ordered TCP bytes do not prove a game applied an event. Local-only is valid when one program owns the experience. These are design tradeoffs, not required group-project features.'))
S.append(content('TEAM / CHOOSE ONE BOUNDARY', 'What should another person be able to observe?', ['Local-only, request/response, or continuing messages?', 'Name one state, one command and one failure.', 'Explain what the screen can honestly confirm.'],
    notes='115-117 minutes. A local-only proposal can name an invalid-input or missing-file failure. Ask teams for one reason for their boundary and one observable check. Connect this to a usable increment, evidence and a next fix in the recommended GitHub Project or an equivalent accessible progress record. Networking, AI and a particular engine remain optional project choices.'))
S.append(content('PFAD / SEPARATE 120-MINUTE TUTORIAL', 'Tutorial: build and inspect the shared scene', [f'[Pinned Week 9 source and complete tutorial]({PRACTICAL})', 'uv run python server.py', 'Two views -> invalid 101 -> disconnect/restart.', 'Change the maximum from 100 to 80; check both transports.', 'Final 20 minutes: tutor review / project support.'], body_size=34,
    notes='117-120 minutes. The two-hour tutorial, not this theory lecture, owns detailed validation/state/await/lock implementation. The README allocates 0-15 setup/pair fallback, 15-35 accepted-message/shared-state trace, 35-55 Python rule edit/check, 55-65 break, 65-85 two clients/polling/continuing updates, 85-100 failure/recovery and 100-120 tutor/project support. Predict and test 79/80/81 after changing the maximum to 80, then restore 100. No extra Canvas upload, login or grade. Core revision covers network roles, duplex/protocols, OSI and the approximate TCP/IP mapping, addressing/subnet/gateway/DHCP, unicast/broadcast/multicast, TCP/UDP, names/ports/diagnostic tool purposes, HTTP/HTTPS/WebRTC/WebSocket, API/REST semantics, framing and application acknowledgement uncertainty; dated news, version memorization, exact command flags and optional LAN/OSC setup are outside core revision. This prepares concepts for the final-course quiz; no new quiz is scheduled at the end of this lecture. Source: https://github.com/sd5913/pfad/tree/6e5637f56c1179f64d170dc29eecbf312bb41a2f/week09/README.md'))
S.append(end('Client to app.\nApp to resources.', 'This week: APIs and WebSockets. Next week: databases.', 'sd5913.github.io/teaching',
    notes='Recap: networking roles and layers; IP/routes/ports; unicast, broadcast and multicast; TCP streams and UDP datagrams; HTTP/REST and persistent WebSocket messages; diagnosis based on evidence; application replies differ from TCP acknowledgements. The tutorial applies those ideas with one Scene, validation, await, a lock, reconnect and uncertain outcomes. Next week the application becomes a client of a database or another resource. A network database typically uses a socket and a database application protocol; SQLite can instead be embedded in the app. Socket endpoints and protocol rules are different ideas. Save database implementation details for Week 10. The source crosswalk records historical networking and moved speech. Generic MCP role-play is not repeated.'))
